Situation:
Enemy interdiction and sporadic indirect fire are disrupting ground lines of communication across the sector. Main Supply Routes are contested in the valley and constrained by a river crossing that requires daily clearance. Alternate mountain passes remain viable but are slower and weather-exposed. Supply Depots Alpha, Bravo, Charlie, Delta, and Echo are dispersed across rear and intermediate areas; stocks vary by class of supply and by day. Forward sustainment requirements focus on ammunition and fuel for Falcon Infantry Battalion and Saber Armored Squadron operating near the line of contact. Movement control has allocated a limited common truck pool; convoy formations require route clearance and escort support. Some shortages may be accepted at a penalty to preserve maneuver tempo.

Mission:
Deliver the required classes of supply from available depots to supported units over the planning window while minimizing total operational cost, to include transportation, vehicle deployment, fixed route security risk, and shortage penalties. Maintain readiness and freedom of action for the supported units.

Execution/Concept:
- Supply and demand: Each depot’s daily issue is constrained by its available stock by supply class. Each unit has daily minimum sustainment requirements by class. If demand cannot be met, deliberate shortfall can be accepted at a defined penalty cost.
- Routing and risk: Movement from a specific depot to a specific unit on a given day requires activating that route for that day. Activating a route incurs a fixed Risk Cost representing escort teams, route clearance, and security posture—this cost is charged once per route-day regardless of the number of vehicles dispatched on that lane.
- Transportation costs: Shipments incur route- and day-specific transport costs. Planning will prioritize lower-risk, lower-cost corridors while balancing timeliness and survivability.
- Vehicles and capacity: All cargo is volumetric; each supply class has an assigned volume factor. Total loaded volume per route-day cannot exceed the payload provided by the number of deployed vehicles and the per-vehicle capacity. A shared fleet cap applies across all depot-to-unit movements each day.
- Operational approach: Concentrate flows through a small set of secure, economical routes to reduce fixed risk overhead. Source from multiple depots when advantageous to meet demand while respecting depot-level constraints. Use shortfall only as a last resort where risk or capacity bars timely delivery. Adjust route activations daily based on threat, clearance status, and cost.

